IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `theft-nouns`

-- Theft Act 1968, sections 30 to 36: spouses and civil partners, civil proceedings,
-- the effect on existing law, commencement and extent. Layer 1.
--
-- Sections 33 (amendments and repeals), 36(1) (the short title) and 36(2) (dots in
-- this text) and the whole of Schedules 2 and 3 are coverage-table items only
-- (NOTES.md). Section 32(2) and section 35(3) are inert for the reasons given where
-- they come.

§ `Sections 30 to 36 - general and consequential provisions`

§§ `Section 30 - spouses and civil partners`

-- src:228 | (1) This Act shall apply in relation to the parties to a marriage, and to property belonging to the wife or husband whether or not by reason of an interest derived from the marriage, as it would apply if they were not married and any such interest subsisted independently of the marriage.
-- src:229 | (2) Subject to subsection (4) below, a person shall have the same right to bring proceedings against that person’s wife or husband for any offence (whether under this Act or otherwise) as if they were not married,and a person bringing any such proceedings shall be competent to give evidence for the prosecution at every stage of the proceedings.
-- src:230 | (3) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:231 | (4) Proceedings shall not be instituted against a person for any offence of stealing or doing unlawful damage to property which at the time of the offence belongs to that person’s wife or husband, or civil partner or for any attempt, incitement or conspiracy to commit such an offence,unless the proceedings are instituted by or with the consent of the Director of Public Prosecutions:
-- src:232 | Provided that—
-- src:233 | (a) this subsection shall not apply to proceedings against a person for an offence—
-- src:234 | (i) if that person is charged with committing the offence jointly with the wife or husband or civil partner; ...
-- src:235 | (ii) if by virtue of any judicial decree or order (wherever made) that person and the wife or husband are at the time of the offence under no obligation to cohabit; . . . or
-- src:236 | (iii) an order (wherever made) is in force providing for the separation of that person and his or her civil partner.
-- src:237 | (b) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:238 | (5) Notwithstanding section 6 of the Prosecution of Offences Act 1979 subsection (4) of this section shall apply—
-- src:239 | (a) to an arrest (if without warrant) made by the wife or husband, or civil partner and
-- src:240 | (b) to a warrant of arrest issued on an information laid by the wife or husband or civil partner .
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 30 (src:228-240)
GIVEN p IS A `Spouse proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 30(4) the step may be taken` p MEANS
    IF `s 30(5) the step is one that subsection (4) applies to` p
       AND p's `the offence is stealing, or doing unlawful damage to, property which at the time belonged to his wife, husband or civil partner, or an attempt, incitement or conspiracy to commit such an offence`
       AND NOT (`s 30(4)(a) the subsection does not apply to the proceedings` p)
    THEN p's `the step is taken by or with the consent of the Director of Public Prosecutions`
    ELSE TRUE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 30(4)(a) (src:233-236)
GIVEN p IS A `Spouse proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 30(4)(a) the subsection does not apply to the proceedings` p MEANS
        "(i)" ... p's `he is charged with committing the offence jointly with his wife, husband or civil partner`
    OR  "(ii)" ... p's `by a judicial decree or order (wherever made) he and his wife or husband were at the time of the offence under no obligation to cohabit`
    OR  "(iii)" ... p's `an order (wherever made) is in force providing for the separation of him and his civil partner`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 30(5) (src:238-240)
GIVEN p IS A `Spouse proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 30(5) the step is one that subsection (4) applies to` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p's `the step taken`
        WHEN `proceedings are instituted against him` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an arrest without warrant made by his wife, husband or civil partner` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a warrant of arrest issued on an information laid by his wife, husband or civil partner` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `some other step` THEN FALSE

-- s 30(2). "Subject to subsection (4)".
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 30(2) (src:229)
GIVEN p IS A `Spouse proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 30(2) his wife or husband may bring proceedings against him as if they were not married` p MEANS
        p's `the person bringing the proceedings is his wife or husband`
    AND `s 30(4) the step may be taken` p

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 30(2) (src:229)
GIVEN p IS A `Spouse proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 30(2) the spouse who brings the proceedings is competent to give evidence for the prosecution` p MEANS
    p's `the person bringing the proceedings is his wife or husband`

-- s 30(1) says the Act applies to the parties to a marriage, and to property
-- belonging to the wife or husband, as it would if they were not married. It adds no
-- rule: `s 5 the property belongs to another` reads neither marriage field of
-- `Belonging facts`, and theft-cases-s30-36.l4 asserts that the answer to `s 1
-- guilty of theft` does not change with them. Section 30(3) and (4)(b) are shown as
-- dots in this text.

§§ `Section 31 - effect on civil proceedings and rights`

-- src:242 | (1) A person shall not be excused, by reason that to do so may incriminate that person or the spouse or civil partner of that person of an offence under this Act—
-- src:243 | (a) from answering any question put to that person in proceedings for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property; or
-- src:244 | (b) from complying with any order made in any such proceedings;
-- src:245 | but no statement or admission made by a person in answering a question put or complying with an order made as aforesaid shall, in proceedings for an offence under this Act, be admissible in evidence against that person or (unless they married or became civil partners after the making of the statement or admission) against the spouse or civil partner of that person.
-- src:246 | (2) Notwithstanding any enactment to the contrary, where property has been stolen or obtained by fraud or other wrongful means, the title to that or any other property shall not be affected by reason only of the conviction of the offender.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 31 (src:242-246)
GIVEN c IS A `Civil proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 31(1) he is not excused from answering the question or complying with the order` c MEANS
        c's `the proceedings are for the recovery or administration of any property, for the execution of any trust or for an account of any property or dealings with property`
    AND c's `he would be excused from the question or the order only because answering or complying may incriminate him or his spouse or civil partner of an offence under this Act`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 31(1) (src:245)
GIVEN c IS A `Civil proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 31(1) the statement or admission cannot be given in evidence against him` c MEANS
        c's `the statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in such proceedings`
    AND c's `it is offered in proceedings for an offence under this Act`
    AND c's `it is offered against him`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 31(1) (src:245)
GIVEN c IS A `Civil proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 31(1) the statement or admission cannot be given in evidence against his spouse or civil partner` c MEANS
        c's `the statement or admission was made in answering a question put, or complying with an order made, in such proceedings`
    AND c's `it is offered in proceedings for an offence under this Act`
    AND c's `it is offered against his spouse or civil partner`
    AND NOT (c's `they married or became civil partners after the statement or admission was made`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 31(2) (src:246)
GIVEN c IS A `Civil proceedings facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 31(2) the conviction alone does not affect the title to the property` c MEANS
        c's `property has been stolen or obtained by fraud or other wrongful means`
    AND c's `the only thing said to affect the title is the conviction of the offender`

§§ `Section 35 - commencement, and the dates the Act carries`

-- The date the Act was passed is the date in square brackets under the long title
-- (line 4 of the text), and the date the Act came into force is in s 35(1). Both are
-- data. The commencement date decides which offences the Act reaches (s 35(1)) and
-- which old offences stay abolished (s 32(1)).
-- src:4 | [26th July 1968]
@ref Theft Act 1968 date of enactment (src:4)
GIVETH A DATE
`the date the Theft Act 1968 was enacted` MEANS Date 26 7 1968

-- src:270 | (1) This Act shall come into force on the 1st January 1969 and, save as otherwise provided by this Act, shall have effect only in relation to offences wholly or partly committed on or after that date.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 35(1) (src:270)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 35(1) the date the Act came into force` MEANS Date 1 1 1969

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 35(1) (src:270)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 35(1) the Act has effect in relation to an offence` d MEANS
    d AT LEAST `s 35(1) the date the Act came into force`

-- s 35(2). Section 27 applies to proceedings for an offence committed before
-- commencement as it would to a corresponding offence under this Act. Section 148
-- of the Powers of Criminal Courts (Sentencing) Act 2000 is not among the inputs, so
-- the second half of s 35(2) is not encoded.
-- src:271 | (2) Section 27 of this Act and section 148 of the Powers of Criminal Courts (Sentencing) Act 2000shall apply in relation to proceedings for an offence committed before the commencement of this Act as they would apply in relation to proceedings for a corresponding offence under this Act, and shall so apply in place of any corresponding enactment repealed by this Act.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 35(2) (src:271)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 35(2) section 27 applies to the offence as it would to a corresponding offence under this Act` d MEANS
    d LESS THAN `s 35(1) the date the Act came into force`

-- src:272 | (3) Subject to subsection (2) above, no repeal or amendment by this Act of any enactment relating to procedure or evidence, or to the jurisdiction or powers of any court, or to the effect of a conviction, shall affect the operation of the enactment in relation to offences committed before the commencement of this Act or to proceedings for any such offence.
-- s 35(3) says that repeals and amendments of procedure, evidence, jurisdiction and
-- effect-of-conviction enactments do not affect their operation for offences
-- committed, or proceedings brought, before commencement. It only preserves the old
-- law, which is not among the inputs, so there is nothing to compute: coverage table,
-- inert.

§§ `Section 32 - effect on existing law`

-- src:248 | (1) The following offences are hereby abolished for all purposes not relating to offences committed before the commencement of this Act, that is to say—
-- src:249 | (a) any offence at common law of larceny, robbery, burglary, receiving stolen property, obtaining property by threats, extortion by colour or office or franchise, false accounting by public officers, concealment of treasure trove and, except as regards offences relating to the public revenue, cheating; and
-- src:250 | (b) any offence under an enactment mentioned in Part I of Schedule 3 to this Act, to the extent to which the offence depends on any section or part of a section included in column 3 of that Schedule;
-- src:251 | but so that the provisions in Schedule 1 to this Act (which preserve with modifications certain offences under the Larcency Act 1861 of taking or killing deer and taking or destroying fish) shall have effect as there set out.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 32(1) (src:248-251)
GIVEN a IS AN `Abolition facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 32(1) the old offence is abolished as regards what he did` a MEANS
        `s 35(1) the Act has effect in relation to an offence` (a's `the last day on which any part of the offence was committed`)
    AND CONSIDER a's `the old offence`
            WHEN `cheating` THEN NOT (a's `the cheating relates to the public revenue`)
            WHEN `none of these` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 32(2) (lines 252 to 254) makes references in other enactments to abolished
-- offences, and to theft, stealing, robbery, blackmail, burglary and handling, mean
-- the corresponding offence under this Act. Those enactments are not among the
-- inputs, so there is nothing to compute: coverage table, inert.

§§ `Section 36 - extent`

-- src:3 | An Act to revise the law of England and Wales as to theft and similar or associated offences, and in connection therewith to make provision as to criminal proceedings by one party to a marriage against the other, and to make certain amendments extending beyond England and Wales in the Post Office Act 1953 and other enactments; and for other purposes connected therewith.
@ref Theft Act 1968 long title (src:3)
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the long title says the Act revises the law of England and Wales` j MEANS
    CONSIDER j
        WHEN `England and Wales` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

-- src:276 | (3) This Act does not extend to Scotland or, . . . to Northern Ireland, except as regards any amendment or repeal which in accordance with section 33 above is to extend to Scotland or Northern Ireland.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 36(3) (src:276)
GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction`
GIVETH AN `Extent of the Act`
`s 36(3) where the Act extends` j MEANS
    CONSIDER j
        WHEN `England and Wales` THEN `the Act extends there`
        WHEN `Scotland` THEN `the Act does not extend there, by section 36(3)`
        WHEN `Northern Ireland` THEN `the Act does not extend there, by section 36(3)`
        WHEN `the Isle of Man` THEN `the text does not say whether the Act extends there`
        WHEN `the Channel Islands` THEN `the text does not say whether the Act extends there`
        WHEN `outside the British postal area` THEN `the text does not say whether the Act extends there`
